Enhanced the existing Jenkins EFS cleanup Python script to automatically remove stale Jenkins workspaces and .terraform directories, helping reduce EFS storage utilization from approximately 512 GiB toward the target of below 250 GiB.

Investigation and Findings

Identified numerous stale Jenkins workspaces older than 90 days, including historical PR builds, @tmp, @2, and @3 directories.

Confirmed the EFS workspace is mounted at /home/jenkins/workspace inside the Jenkins Docker container.

Determined that the original script only removed .terraform directories and did not clean up stale Jenkins workspaces.

Identified unnecessary filesystem scanning overhead in the original implementation.

Cleanup Script Updates

Added automatic deletion of Jenkins workspaces older than 90 days using --execute.

Retained existing recursive .terraform cleanup functionality.

Added dry-run mode to preview directories before deletion.

Added protection against deleting the workspace root, symbolic links, or directories outside the configured path.

Prevented duplicate cleanup of .terraform directories located inside stale workspaces.

Removed the initial full-filesystem size calculation to improve EFS performance.

Retained MAX_WORKERS = 8 for concurrent cleanup operations.

Enhanced logging to display identified directories, deletion results, and estimated storage reclaimed.
